q('For serious invasive MRSA infection with a vancomycin MIC assumed to be 1 mg/L, which exposure target is recommended?', 'A daily AUC of approximately 400–600 mg·h/L using an appropriate monitoring approach',[
 ('A trough above 25 mg/L in every patient regardless of AUC','This can increase nephrotoxicity.'),('An AUC above 1,000 as the routine safety target','Excess exposure raises toxicity risk.'),('No monitoring during prolonged therapy or unstable renal function','Exposure and renal risk need assessment.')],
 'AUC-guided dosing balances efficacy and kidney safety', '**The consensus target concerns serious invasive MRSA infection.**',['AUC estimation can use validated Bayesian or concentration-based methods.','The MIC method and clinical context affect interpretation.'], '**Do not chase a high trough when exposure is already adequate.**',['Monitor renal function and interacting nephrotoxins.','Reassess dosing as clearance changes.'],[['Target','AUC 400–600 with MIC assumption 1'],['Goal','Efficacy with less nephrotoxicity'],['Monitoring','Exposure and changing kidney function']],[VA])
q('A patient on prolonged gentamicin develops rising creatinine and new imbalance or hearing symptoms. Which toxicity should be considered?', 'Aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity',[
 ('An expected harmless effect requiring no assessment','These can be serious toxicities.'),('Only a skin allergy because all symptoms are immune-mediated','Aminoglycosides have direct organ-toxicity risks.'),('Proof that a higher accumulating trough is needed','Accumulation can worsen harm.')],
 'Renal and inner-ear toxicity are central aminoglycoside limitations', '**Risk increases with exposure and vulnerable renal function.**',['Ototoxicity can involve vestibular or auditory function.','Other causes of AKI or imbalance still require assessment.'], '**Review necessity, drug exposure and alternatives promptly.**',['Use appropriate monitoring and dose adjustment.','Avoid unnecessary concurrent nephrotoxins.'],[['Kidney','AKI risk'],['Ear','Vestibular/auditory injury'],['Response','Exposure and regimen review']],[GE],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why does linezolid require careful review of serotonergic co-medications?', 'It is a reversible nonselective monoamine-oxidase inhibitor and can contribute to serotonin toxicity',[
 ('It lowers every serotonin concentration to zero','That is not its mechanism.'),('It has no interactions because oral bioavailability is high','Bioavailability does not eliminate pharmacodynamic interactions.'),('It directly blocks only bacterial folate synthesis','Its antibacterial action is ribosomal and the MAO effect is a separate interaction concern.')],
 'A useful antibacterial can have clinically relevant host-enzyme effects', '**Serotonergic combinations need a benefit-risk and monitoring plan.**',['Urgent infection treatment and psychiatric-drug interruption both have consequences.','Do not make unsupervised changes from a drug name alone.'], '**Assess symptoms and the exact co-medication list.**',['Agitation, clonus, autonomic change and hyperthermia can be clues.','Use specialist advice when alternatives or washout decisions are complex.'],[['Drug property','Reversible MAO inhibition'],['Interaction','Serotonergic toxicity risk'],['Plan','Individual assessment and monitoring']],[LI])
q('Which laboratory monitoring is particularly relevant during daptomycin therapy?', 'Creatine kinase, with closer review when muscle symptoms, renal impairment or other myotoxic drugs are present',[
 ('An isolated INR as a complete myopathy assessment','It does not assess muscle injury.'),('A blood eosinophil count as the only necessary safety test','Daptomycin has several possible toxicities.'),('No testing unless the patient becomes unable to walk','Earlier recognition is important.')],
 'Daptomycin can injure skeletal muscle', '**Myopathy and rhabdomyolysis are recognized risks.**',['Routine CPK/CK monitoring is recommended.','Renal impairment can require more frequent review.'], '**Ask about pain and weakness rather than relying on numbers alone.**',['Review statins and other relevant agents.','New pulmonary symptoms also require assessment for eosinophilic pneumonia or another cause.'],[['Monitoring','CK/CPK'],['Symptoms','Myalgia or weakness'],['Risk review','Renal function and other myotoxic agents']],[DA])
q('A patient with worsening renal function develops confusion and myoclonus while receiving cefepime. What is an important possibility?', 'Cefepime-associated neurotoxicity, including possible nonconvulsive seizures',[
 ('A finding that can occur only with a penicillin allergy','This toxicity is not limited to an allergic mechanism.'),('Proof that more cefepime is required without reviewing exposure','Accumulation may be the problem.'),('An impossible drug effect if the first few doses were tolerated','Risk can emerge as clearance changes.')],
 'Changing renal clearance can turn an appropriate dose into an excessive exposure', '**Cefepime can cause encephalopathy, myoclonus and seizures.**',['Renal impairment increases risk.','Neurotoxicity can occasionally occur despite apparently adjusted dosing.'], '**Stop/review the suspected drug and assess alternative causes urgently.**',['EEG can identify nonconvulsive seizures when indicated.','Provide an effective alternative infection regimen.'],[['Risk','Renal accumulation'],['Phenotype','Encephalopathy/myoclonus/seizures'],['Action','Urgent drug and neurological assessment']],[CE],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient develops Achilles tendon pain during fluoroquinolone therapy. What is the best principle?', 'Stop the suspected fluoroquinolone, avoid tendon strain and arrange clinical review with alternative treatment if needed',[
 ('Continue exercise to prevent tendon stiffness without review','This can increase rupture risk.'),('Ignore the symptom unless fever recurs','The symptom can be a serious drug adverse effect.'),('Double the dose to overcome presumed bacterial resistance','The symptom does not establish resistance.')],
 'Fluoroquinolones have important noninfectious adverse effects', '**Tendinopathy and tendon rupture can occur.**',['Peripheral neuropathy and other potentially persistent effects are also recognized.','Age, steroids and organ disease can modify risk.'], '**Use these agents when the expected benefit justifies the risk.**',['Choose an effective alternative for the infection when appropriate.','Document the reaction and counsel on warning symptoms.'],[['Warning','Tendon pain'],['Action','Stop and review'],['Prevention','Appropriate indication and risk assessment']],[FQ],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An older patient with CKD taking an ACE inhibitor develops hyperkalemia after TMP-SMX. Which drug contribution is important?', 'Trimethoprim can reduce renal potassium excretion and increase hyperkalemia risk',[
 ('TMP-SMX always lowers potassium','The effect can be the opposite.'),('The antibiotic cannot affect electrolytes at treatment doses','Hyperkalemia can occur at recommended doses.'),('A stable baseline potassium removes the need for follow-up','The risk emerges after exposure and changing renal function.')],
 'An antibiotic interaction can create an electrolyte emergency', '**Renal impairment and other potassium-raising drugs increase vulnerability.**',['Assess the ECG and severity promptly.','Review the antibiotic and the full medication list.'], '**Monitor potassium and renal function when risk warrants it.**',['Use an alternative regimen when the benefit-risk balance requires.','Do not attribute every creatinine change to harmless secretion effects without assessment.'],[['Drug','Trimethoprim'],['Amplifiers','CKD and ACE inhibitor'],['Monitoring','Potassium and renal function']],[TM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient’s penicillin-allergy label comes only from childhood diarrhea without rash, respiratory symptoms or systemic reaction. What is appropriate?', 'Risk-stratify the history and consider a supervised delabeling pathway rather than assume true IgE allergy',[
 ('Diarrhea alone proves anaphylaxis','It is usually an intolerance-type history.'),('Avoid every beta-lactam permanently without review','An inaccurate label can compromise treatment.'),('Perform an unsupervised home challenge regardless of history','Testing needs the appropriate clinical pathway.')],
 'An allergy label should describe the actual reaction', '**Many reported penicillin allergies are not persistent immune allergy.**',['Timing, symptoms and severity determine risk.','Selected low-risk patients can undergo direct oral challenge in an appropriate setting.'], '**Document the outcome accurately.**',['Severe delayed reactions are a different category.','Do not use a low-risk pathway when the history suggests anaphylaxis or severe organ/skin injury.'],[['History','Define the reaction'],['Low risk','May qualify for supervised challenge'],['Benefit','Restore suitable first-line options']],[PA,PS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient had penicillin-associated Stevens–Johnson syndrome. Which principle is appropriate?', 'Do not use a routine low-risk oral-challenge pathway; obtain specialist guidance and avoid the implicated high-risk exposure',[
 ('Treat it as simple gastrointestinal intolerance','It is a severe delayed reaction.'),('A negative immediate skin test alone makes rechallenge automatically safe','Immediate IgE testing does not resolve severe delayed-reaction risk.'),('Give a trial dose without monitoring because the event was remote','The severity changes the safety pathway.')],
 'Severe delayed reactions differ from immediate allergy and benign intolerance', '**SJS/TEN history requires a high-risk specialist approach.**',['Skin testing for immediate allergy does not exclude severe T-cell-mediated disease.','The original drug and related-drug decisions need careful review.'], '**Select an effective safe alternative treatment.**',['Record the phenotype clearly.','Avoid vague labels that conceal the actual severe reaction.'],[['Phenotype','Severe delayed reaction'],['Not suitable','Routine low-risk challenge'],['Plan','Specialist drug-allergy guidance']],[PS])
q('A patient develops new significant diarrhea after broad-spectrum antibiotics. Which stewardship principle is appropriate?', 'Assess for CDI and other causes, and stop or narrow unnecessary precipitating antibiotics',[
 ('Treat every diarrhea episode with more broad-spectrum antibiotics automatically','This can worsen harm and miss the cause.'),('Ignore symptoms because antibiotic-associated diarrhea is always benign','CDI can be severe.'),('Continue all agents indefinitely because they were started in hospital','Ongoing indication must be reassessed.')],
 'Antimicrobial harm is part of the treatment review', '**Antibiotics can disrupt colonization resistance.**',['Apply CDI testing to an appropriate clinical syndrome.','Laxatives and other causes also need consideration.'], '**Reduce avoidable exposure while treating the established infection.**',['Do not stop a necessary agent without an effective plan.','Use source and susceptibility information to narrow safely.'],[['New diarrhea','Investigate clinically'],['Unnecessary agents','Stop/narrow'],['Needed treatment','Maintain effective focused coverage']],[CD],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Why is ceftriaxone alone not an adequate definitive treatment for enterococcal endocarditis?', 'Enterococci have intrinsic poor susceptibility to cephalosporin monotherapy, despite a role for ceftriaxone in selected synergistic combinations',[
 ('Ceftriaxone always becomes active alone whenever the infection is in a valve','The site does not remove intrinsic resistance.'),('Ampicillin–ceftriaxone synergy proves ceftriaxone monotherapy is adequate','Combination effects are not equivalent to single-agent activity.'),('Every Enterococcus has the same susceptibility to all other antibiotics','Species and resistance vary.')],
 'A combination regimen cannot be decomposed into interchangeable single drugs', '**Selected E. faecalis regimens use complementary beta-lactam target effects.**',['That does not make cephalosporin monotherapy appropriate.','Ampicillin susceptibility and species matter.'], '**Use organism-specific endovascular guidance.**',['Check high-level aminoglycoside resistance when synergy is being considered.','Balance kidney toxicity and validated combinations.'],[['Monotherapy','Ceftriaxone inadequate'],['Selected combination','Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone'],['Selection','Species and susceptibility']],[EC])
q('An infected prosthetic focus persists despite an in-vitro-active antibiotic. What is a key reason to reassess source control?', 'Biofilm and devitalized material can prevent eradication despite laboratory susceptibility',[
 ('A susceptible MIC guarantees cure regardless of anatomy','It does not account for every source problem.'),('Antibiotic concentration is the only possible cause of failure','Source, host and diagnostic factors also matter.'),('Every implant must be removed without any clinical assessment','The intervention is individualized to site and risk.')],
 'The microbiology report does not describe the whole infected structure', '**Foreign material can sustain a protected bacterial reservoir.**',['Drainage, debridement or removal may be necessary.','Procedural risk and functional needs affect the plan.'], '**Coordinate antimicrobial and anatomical treatment.**',['Assess persistence with cultures and suitable imaging.','Avoid indefinite empirical escalation without identifying the barrier to cure.'],[['Microbe','In-vitro activity'],['Source','Biofilm or devitalized tissue'],['Plan','Combined medical/procedural strategy']],[EC,AM])
q('In severe infection with renal impairment, how should loading and maintenance doses be considered?', 'Assess them separately: a suitable loading dose establishes prompt exposure, while maintenance reflects clearance and subsequent monitoring',[
 ('Reduce every first dose automatically to the smallest maintenance dose','This can underdose an expanded distribution volume.'),('Renal impairment never affects antimicrobial dosing','It affects clearance of many agents.'),('One fixed dose fits every critical illness and dialysis state','Patient and modality differences matter.')],
 'Distribution and elimination determine different parts of a dosing plan', '**The loading dose primarily relates to target concentration and distribution volume.**',['Maintenance depends substantially on clearance.','Drug-specific safety can modify the general principle.'], '**Use a validated regimen and reassess changing physiology.**',['Dialysis and augmented clearance can alter exposure.','Use therapeutic monitoring where appropriate.'],[['Loading','Prompt target exposure'],['Maintenance','Clearance-dependent'],['Reassessment','Renal course, dialysis and levels']],[SS,AM])
q('What must be established before switching a serious bacterial infection from IV to oral therapy?', 'Clinical suitability, source control, adequate absorption and a susceptible oral agent with appropriate exposure for the infection',[
 ('A lower pill price alone','Cost does not establish efficacy.'),('Any oral antibiotic regardless of susceptibility','The oral regimen must be active.'),('A negative culture alone despite ongoing shock and obstruction','Clinical stability and source control remain necessary.')],
 'An oral route can be effective when the drug and clinical conditions fit', '**IV treatment is not inherently superior once suitable oral therapy can achieve the required exposure.**',['Some syndromes have validated early-switch pathways.','Others require specific specialist criteria or prolonged IV treatment.'], '**Use disease-specific evidence rather than a universal route rule.**',['Confirm adherence and follow-up access.','Document dose, duration and warning symptoms.'],[['Patient','Stable and able to absorb'],['Drug','Active and adequately exposed'],['Syndrome','Evidence-based switch criteria']],[UT,AM])

